  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Heavy rainfall arrives late Tuesday, continuing \novernight and through the evening on Wednesday. This will lead to \npotential street and flash flooding throughout that period. The \nheaviest rain in eastern New England is expected on Wednesday, with \nstreet flooding possible but less favored than further inland where \na Flood Watch is in effect. \n\nThe focus for this week's forecast continues to be on an anomalously \ndeep mid-level low and the associated occluding surface low that \ntogether will bring impressive moisture and dynamics to southern New \nEngland over the next 48 hours. The mid level low digs into NY late \ntoday, becoming closed off and very sluggish to go anywhere thanks \nto a persistent blocking pattern. This will continue to plague us \nthrough the work week as this low languishes over the northeast, \never so slowly moving east and keeping unsettled weather around \nthrough Friday. That being said, by far the best moisture and \ndynamics line up later tonight through Wednesday evening. We'll have \na very moist airmass ahead of the frontal boundary with PWATs up to \n1.7\". Expecting impressive dynamics as well with strong mid level \ndivergence and upper diffluence along with low level convergence \nfrom a 35-45kt LLJ. This will produce deep layer ascent leading to \nwidespread, at times heavy, rainfall. \n\nExpected rainfall totals have not changed significantly since \nyesterday, generally 2 to 3 inches of rain with locally higher \namounts. The most likely location of the higher totals will be in \nthe orographically favored interior of western MA and CT where a \nFlood Watch remains in effect. Easterly flow into the Berkshires is \nlikely to enhance rainfall totals, while potential for training \nstorms (continually moving over the same location) leads to \nincreasingly likely issues with flooding. Timing for the heaviest \nrain would begin as early as this afternoon for interior SNE, while \neastern MA is likely to stay dry until late Tuesday night/Wednesday\nmorning. Heavy rain will eventually move into these areas, in \neastern MA and RI during the day on Wednesday. Some instability \nexists throughout with 500-1000 J/kg of CAPE which could lead to\nsome embedded thunder and convectively enhanced localized \ndownpours. Currently think flooding concerns are limited to \nstreet flooding (urban/poor drainage variety) as no rivers are \ncurrently progged to rise to flood stage. \n\nPerpetual cloudcover and easterly flow off the cool ocean will keep \ntemperatures below normal, only reaching the low 70s on Wednesday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Periodic rain chances continue especially on \nThursday, but may linger into Friday. \n\nPreviously mentioned vertically stacked low continues to slowly \nmeander east and while the best dynamics are no longer present, \nPWATs remain elevated. This, together with the mid level PVA/mid \nlevel shortwaves rounding the trough will keep scattered showers \naround Thursday and even into Friday, though more scattered to \nisolated. Temperatures continue to be depressed by clouds and E/NE \nflow, in the low to mid 70s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...An uncertain weekend forecast but trends point to a \nbuilding surface high pressure and rising mid level heights which \nwould lead to a drier period than earlier in the week.\n\nThe synoptic pattern begins to finally show signs of breaking down \nas ridging downstream is displaced and high pressure tries to build \nover the northeast with some mid level height rises. Guidance is \nstill unsure of how persistent this ridging will be, so confidence \nis low, but for now things at the very least look less unsettled \nthan the work week. No anomolous warm ups on the horizon.",
